// ==========================================================================
// data/planes/cessna172/avl.jsonc for the Cessna 172 source analysis, generated -- do not hand-edit.
//
// 1. SOLVER, MESH, FLIGHT CONDITION
//    Solver: AVL, via aid.avl_io.run_avl_full for the stability sweep and
//    aid.avl_controls.avl_controls for the control derivatives, both from the
//    sibling package imported from ../USAF_DATCOM/AircraftIntuitiveDesign/Python/src, a path relative to this
//    repository's root so the bytes do not depend on which mount point the repo is reached
//    through; see provenance.aid_src.
//    Mesh: 10 spanwise x 10 chordwise.  aid/avl_controls.py's own default, so the
//    stability run and the control run agree.  AVL keeps only WG, HT and VT (nwing <= 3),
//    so NP[0], which tornado.jsonc keeps in its lattice, is NOT in this file.
//    Two runs, because run_avl_full's .sb lists no deflected surface at all and therefore carries
//    no control columns (measured); avl_controls writes a CONTROL card for every legal surface,
//    deflects all of them by the same delta, and reads the deflection columns out of its own .sb.
//    That run is also the one whose aileron CONTROL card carries all seven numbers, which is what
//    makes AVL read the trailing -1 as the duplicate sign instead of as a hinge component.
//    Evaluated at alpha = 4 deg, the middle entry of AERO.ALSCHD, as aid/tornado_io.py::_build_state picks it. run_avl_full also
//    solves an unconstrained case, which AVL trims to zero lift; it is recorded in
//    provenance.reference_case and NOT used, so every slot here describes the alpha = 4 deg
//    condition the other three files use.
//    Flight condition from the source AERO: Mach 0.03, altitude 0.0 ft, betha 0.
//    AID hands AVL's run card `mach * atm['a'] / 3.28084` = 10.207345 as the `v` command,
//    and Sref/Cref/Bref are written in FEET (24.000 / 2.0000 / 12.000), so AVL's solver speed is 10.2073 ft/s
//    = 3.1112 m/s in this model's frame -- the same reading tornado.jsonc uses, and the
//    reason both files carry a 7.42940x rate-derivative caveat.  The same expression in
//    SI is the PHYSICAL speed 10.2073 m/s, which is what flow5's SI deck used instead.  Both are in
//    provenance.flight_condition.  The trimmed cruise speed is 23.1144 m/s.
//
// 2. THE SOURCE PLANFORM IS A CESSNA 172 AT ROUGHLY 1/3 LINEAR SCALE
//    span 12 ft against a real 36.1 ft, chord 2 ft against 4.89 ft, area 24 ft^2
//    against 174 ft^2.  Wing loading is therefore 6.67 lbf/ft^2 where a real C172 is
//    13.2.  Nothing in this file may be compared with a full-scale figure.
//
// 3. MASS
//    mass_kg = 72.574 is derived from the source's own AERO.WT = 5 slugs:
//    floor(AERO.WT * 32.0 ft/s^2 / 2.2046226 lbf/kg * 1000)/1000 = 72.574 kg, truncated from
//    72.5747799 kg, i.e. 160 lbf.  A slug is a mass unit, so the
//    source's WT converts instead as AERO.WT * SLUG_TO_KG = 72.96951 kg; the plan's figure assumes g = 32.0 ft/s^2, which
//    appears nowhere in the source (its own G_FT_S2 is 32.174), so the plan is 0.54 % low and
//    everything derived from the weight is too.
//    This is NOT a geometrically similar 1/3 scale C172, which would weigh about 53.5 kg, so no
//    scaling of a published full-scale number is self-consistent and none was attempted.
//    ixx, iyy, izz come from radius-of-gyration rules on THIS mass and THIS span (see below);
//    ixz = 0 and he = 0.  All four Cessna files derive mass and inertia identically from the same
//    geometry, so they are consistent with each other and with tornado.jsonc.
//    xcg and xcg_ref are BOTH 0.0 here, where tornado.jsonc carries -0.896112 for both, and that
//    difference is deliberate rather than an oversight: these three solvers report about the CG
//    itself (verified in item 5), so the CG's offset FROM THE MOMENT REFERENCE is zero, while for
//    Tornado it is the 2.94 ft station shift.  The schema's own `Cm + Cz*(xcgref - xcg)` term is
//    identically zero either way, because xcg == xcg_ref on every reachable path.
//
// 4. UNIT CONVERSIONS APPLIED, AND THE ONES NOT TRUSTED
//    length: ft x 0.3048 = 0.3048 m; angle: degrees x 57.29577951308232 (= DEG_TO_RAD) per radian.
//    AVL'S OWN UNITS ARE ALREADY CORRECT for this file and NOTHING had to be
//    repaired: its stability derivatives are per radian, its .sb deflection
//    columns are PER DEGREE and are multiplied by 57.29577951308232 here, and its
//    p-hat/q-hat/r-hat already use its own V with the reference lengths in the
//    same units.  aid.handbook_pass._per_rad and aid.stability._per_deg were NOT
//    used anywhere on this path: AVL does not go through them.
//    Every array in this file is per radian and every length is SI.  numpy bridge: numpy.trapezoid present; no alias bound
//    cx[0] -- the parasite drag slot, Cd0 in a drag build-up -- = -0.0170028,
//    from AVL's own CDtot - CDind at the alpha = 0 run case (0.0187 - 0.0016972).
//    AVL's reported CDvis is 0.017 and the #CDp card it was given was
//    0.017219 (the source planform's own DATCOM-computed WG.CD0); all three agree
//    to the four decimal places AVL prints them at.
//
// 5. THE MOMENT REFERENCE SHIFT
//    NO MOMENT SHIFT WAS APPLIED, and that is verified rather than assumed.  aid.avl_io writes the
//    geometry's reference station from AERO.XCG into AVL's `#Xref Yref Zref` card, and this
//    converter read it back out of the file it wrote and got Xref = 2.9400 ft,
//    against AERO.XCG = 2.94 ft.  A mismatch raises rather than
//    proceeding, because every moment in this file is used unshifted.  dx = dy = dz = 0, so
//    cm[1], cm[0], cm[2], cmq[0], cn[0], cnp[0], cnr[0] are used as they stand.  Contrast
//    tornado.jsonc, whose ref_point is 2.94 ft AHEAD of the CG and is corrected by dx = -0.896112 m.
//
// 6. SIGN CONVENTION
//    cz is the body +z force coefficient with z DOWN, so cz = -CL and cz[1] is NEGATIVE for a
//    stable aircraft (CL_alpha = -cz[1] > 0), and cm[1] is negative too.  That identity is
//    load-bearing; it is what makes data/planes/linear/morelli.json's cz[1] = -4.5 CORRECT, not a
//    defect of that file.
//    data/planes/linear/morelli.json is the sign-convention authority wherever it disagrees with
//    data/planes/f16/morelli.json.  It WINS on cm[1] (-0.8 against +0.0466) and on cnp[0] (-0.03
//    against +0.0268), where a positive value would be statically unstable.  It LOSES on cnda[0]
//    (+0.01 against -0.0335), where adverse yaw fixes the sign.  A reader must not assume the two
//    sets share a convention: this file and linear/morelli.json agree on cz[1] and disagree with
//    the F-16 set on cm[1] and cnp[0].
//    AVL PRINTS ITS OWN CONVENTION, and it is the same one plane/dynamics.py uses: its .sb
//    header reads "Standard axis orientation, X fwd, Z down", with positive Cl rolling
//    right, positive Cm nose up and positive Cn yaw right.  So NO axis mapping is
//    applied in this file.  Which slots, if any, the `cz = -CL` identity still negates is derived:
//    5 negations survive, and none is a frame conversion.  Each is a SIGN-CONVENTION identity that the schema
//    itself requires -- `cz = -CL` on cz[1], cz[0] and cz[5]; `cx = -CD` on cx[0]; `czq[0] = -CL_q` -- its body +z axis points down, not a change
//    of axis.  Listed from provenance.mapping:
//      cz[1] from AVL .st CLA at alpha = 4 deg = 5.007369 -> -5.007369 per radian
//      cz[0] from AVL .st CLtot at alpha = 0 deg = 0.168880 -> -0.168880
//      cx[0] from AVL .st CDtot - CDind at alpha = 0 deg = 0.017003 -> -0.017003
//      czq[0] from AVL .st CLq at alpha = 4 deg = 9.094834 -> -9.094834 per radian
//      cz[5] from aid.avl_controls[elevator].CL = 0.006592 per degree -> -0.377694 per radian
//    The roll and yaw RATE slots that identity does not touch are decided by the invariant
//    table instead, and both are printed here from the .st channel this adapter reads rather
//    than from a similarly named one (`normalised` is the solver's own sign already on the
//    invariant side of zero, `flipped` is the table's reversal):
//      clp[0]    cl_p    AVL .st Clp at alpha = 4 deg = -0.470451 -> normalised
//      cnp[0]    cn_p    AVL .st Cnp at alpha = 4 deg = +0.007108 -> flipped
//    A table flip and a schema identity are different things with different justifications, and
//    neither is a frame conversion.  Every sign is decided mechanically by aero_convert.units.normalise_sign
//    against SIGN_INVARIANTS; the slots it flipped are listed in provenance.flipped, computed from
//    this run's own mapping.
//    cm0 (Cm0) carries NO invariant -- the two in-tree reference files disagree on it and its
//    sign is tail rigging, not a derivable convention -- so it is written through untouched and is
//    recorded as `not-normalised` in provenance.not_normalised.  Three provenance states exist and
//    all three are recorded: `missing` (field None, slot 0.0), `flipped` (sign normalised against
//    its invariant row) and `not-normalised` (cm0).
//
//    A CAVEAT on the 9 genuinely per-phat / per-qhat / per-rhat slots:
//    cyp[0], cyr[0], czq[0], cmq[0], clp[0], clr[0], cnp[0], cnr[0], cxq[0].
//      NOT rescaled, as in tornado.jsonc. This solver quotes every rate
//      derivative per p*b/(2V), q*cbar/(2V) or r*b/(2V) at the SOURCE
//      Mach-0.03 speed V = 3.1112 m/s, while f16/aero_morelli.py forms phat =
//      p*b_m/(2*V) at the trimmed cruise V = 23.1144 m/s. Restoring the
//      solver's physical dC/dq would mean multiplying the 9 per-hat slots by
//      V_trim/V_source = 7.42940, because czq[0]*cbar/(2*V_trim) must equal
//      CL_q*cbar/(2*V_source); the model's rate-damping terms are that much
//      weaker than the solver implies. Note the plan writes that factor as
//      AS_source/V_trim, which is the INVERSE of the direction that restores
//      dC/dq; nothing is rescaled here either way, so the question is only
//      ever a note.
//    cy[1] and cy[2] are NOT in that list: they are cy_da and cy_dr, the aileron
//    and rudder side force per radian of CONTROL DEFLECTION, which no
//    flight-condition speed touches.
//
//    NO SLOT IS DECLARED ABSENT: AVL produces all 25, including cnda[0] and
//    cy[1], which DATCOM cannot, so provenance.missing is empty.
//      (none: no field of this solver is zeroed and declared -- it produced a value
//       for every one of them in the schema)

//    SEPARATELY, 7 SLOT(S) ARE FILLED BUT FLIPPED -- these are NOT absent:
//    they carry a real solver value whose sign the invariant table reversed, so
//    the solver's own sign sits on the other side of zero: cd_q, cl_dr, cn_da, cn_dr, cn_p, cy_da, cy_dr
//    Every one is in the slot table above with state `flipped`.
//
// 7. PER-SLOT PROVENANCE (all 19 arrays; real solver output vs zeroed)
//   cz[1]     cl_alpha   normalised     solver AVL .st CLA at alpha = 4 deg = 5.00737 -> mapped -5.00737 -> written -5.00737
//   cm[1]     cm_alpha   normalised     solver AVL .st Cma at alpha = 4 deg = -1.42182 -> mapped -1.42182 -> written -1.42182
//   cz[0]     cl0        normalised     solver AVL .st CLtot at alpha = 0 deg = 0.16888 -> mapped -0.16888 -> written -0.16888
//   cm[0]     cm0        not-normalised solver AVL .st Cmtot at alpha = 0 deg = 0.02144 -> mapped 0.02144 -> written 0.02144
//   cx[0]     cd0        normalised     solver AVL .st CDtot - CDind at alpha = 0 deg = 0.0170028 -> mapped -0.0170028 -> written -0.0170028
//   cl[0]     cl_beta    normalised     solver AVL .st Clb at alpha = 4 deg = -0.045756 -> mapped -0.045756 -> written -0.045756
//   cy[0]     cy_beta    normalised     solver AVL .st CYb at alpha = 4 deg = -0.374191 -> mapped -0.374191 -> written -0.374191
//   cn[0]     cn_beta    normalised     solver AVL .st Cnb at alpha = 4 deg = 0.175872 -> mapped 0.175872 -> written 0.175872
//   clp[0]    cl_p       normalised     solver AVL .st Clp at alpha = 4 deg = -0.470451 -> mapped -0.470451 -> written -0.470451
//   clr[0]    cl_r       normalised     solver AVL .st Clr at alpha = 4 deg = 0.135087 -> mapped 0.135087 -> written 0.135087
//   cyp[0]    cy_p       normalised     solver AVL .st CYp at alpha = 4 deg = -0.151727 -> mapped -0.151727 -> written -0.151727
//   cyr[0]    cy_r       normalised     solver AVL .st CYr at alpha = 4 deg = 0.43906 -> mapped 0.43906 -> written 0.43906
//   cnp[0]    cn_p       flipped        solver AVL .st Cnp at alpha = 4 deg = 0.007108 -> mapped 0.007108 -> written -0.007108
//   cnr[0]    cn_r       normalised     solver AVL .st Cnr at alpha = 4 deg = -0.211615 -> mapped -0.211615 -> written -0.211615
//   cmq[0]    cm_q       normalised     solver AVL .st Cmq at alpha = 4 deg = -18.6138 -> mapped -18.6138 -> written -18.6138
//   czq[0]    cl_q       normalised     solver AVL .st CLq at alpha = 4 deg = 9.09483 -> mapped -9.09483 -> written -9.09483
//   cxq[0]    cd_q       flipped        solver AVL .sb CXq (line 46) = -0.140889 -> mapped -0.140889 -> written 0.140889
//   clda[0]   cl_da      normalised     solver aid.avl_controls[aileron].Cl = -0.004549 -> mapped -0.260639 -> written -0.260639
//   cy[1]     cy_da      flipped        solver aid.avl_controls[aileron].CY = -0.000959 -> mapped -0.0549467 -> written 0.0549467
//   cnda[0]   cn_da      flipped        solver aid.avl_controls[aileron].Cn = 9.4e-05 -> mapped 0.0053858 -> written -0.0053858
//   cz[5]     cl_de      normalised     solver aid.avl_controls[elevator].CL = 0.006592 -> mapped -0.377694 -> written -0.377694
//   cm[2]     cm_de      normalised     solver aid.avl_controls[elevator].Cm = -0.020322 -> mapped -1.16436 -> written -1.16436
//   cldr[0]   cl_dr      flipped        solver aid.avl_controls[rudder].Cl = -0.000876 -> mapped -0.0501911 -> written 0.0501911
//   cy[2]     cy_dr      flipped        solver aid.avl_controls[rudder].CY = -0.006586 -> mapped -0.37735 -> written 0.37735
//   cndr[0]   cn_dr      flipped        solver aid.avl_controls[rudder].Cn = 0.003556 -> mapped 0.203744 -> written -0.203744
//      cx[1] (CD_alpha) and cx[3] (CD_de) are real schema slots: LINEAR_INDEX
//      lists cx's linear indexes as (0, 1, 3) and f16/aero_morelli.py
//      evaluates Cx0 = cx[0] + cx[1]*alpha + ... + cx[3]*de, so the flight
//      model reads both terms. They ship 0.0 because DerivativeSet has no
//      field for either derivative and aero_convert/morelli.py's SLOT_MAP
//      maps only ('cx', ((0, 'cd0'),)) -- a MAPPING gap, not a schema gap.
//      The linear slots this file leaves at 0.0 without a provenance.missing
//      entry are: cx[1] and cx[3]
//    provenance.zeroed_slots.CD_alpha carries the same fact in full.
//    THE REST OF THIS SOLVER'S RAW, UNCONVERTED OUTPUT IS IN geometry.jsonc, under raw_cross_checks.avl
//    -- the Fortran's own for006 table and AID's pre-correction handbook rows for
//    DATCOM, the per-alpha .st rows for AVL, the deck and the polar for flow5.
//    A model file's provenance.mapping carries only the one raw number behind each
//    slot, so that block is what makes the rest re-derivable without re-running.
//
// 8. HOW TO REGENERATE THIS FILE
//    cd python && python3 scripts/build_cessna172_aero.py
//    Nothing else writes these files; the sibling analysis and the sibling aid tree
//    are read-only inputs and all solver scratch goes to a temporary directory.
// ==========================================================================
